      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Frans Pretorius",
        "John G. Baker",
        "Manuela Campanelli"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Making binary black-hole mergers calculable in full general relativity turned a central theoretical problem into a practical foundation for gravitational-wave astronomy. The method enabled a major new way to observe the universe.",
        "attribution": "Pretorius achieved the breakthrough in stable simulations; Baker and Campanelli led independent numerical breakthroughs that established and extended the approach. Credit is substantial, though the work drew on broader teams.",
        "maturity": "The methods have been confirmed through extensive numerical use and their connection to observed merger signals.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The division of credit among the independent breakthroughs and later community-wide development needs careful historical assessment."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B006",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Hidetoshi Katori",
        "Jun Ye"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks transformed frequency standards and precision measurement, with reach into tests of fundamental physics as well as timekeeping.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye independently developed leading clock approaches and drove their experimental realization. The two-person attribution is unusually clear for an instrumental advance of this scale.",
        "maturity": "The technique has been repeatedly demonstrated and refined into clocks with extraordinary accuracy and stability.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The relative weight of the initial concepts, experimental realization, and subsequent precision gains should be assessed."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B002",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Francis Halzen",
        "Albrecht Karle",
        "Christian Spiering"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "IceCube's detection of high-energy astrophysical neutrinos opened a new observational channel on extreme cosmic sources and established neutrino astronomy beyond the solar-neutrino regime.",
        "attribution": "Halzen was central to conceiving and building the observatory; Karle and Spiering made major contributions to its design, construction, and scientific leadership. The discovery itself belongs to a large collaboration.",
        "maturity": "The signal is established through sustained observations, and the field has developed beyond the initial discovery.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The contribution of the named leaders relative to the many essential IceCube collaborators requires disciplined attribution."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B003",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Charles L. Kane",
        "Eugene J. Mele",
        "Laurens W. Molenkamp"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The quantum spin Hall effect and topological insulators revealed robust electronic states protected by topology, creating a major direction in condensed-matter physics.",
        "attribution": "Kane and Mele established the theoretical framework, while Molenkamp's experiments demonstrated the quantum spin Hall state in HgTe quantum wells. The theory-to-experiment attribution is coherent.",
        "maturity": "The original effect was experimentally established and the broader topological-materials field has been intensively explored.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The award boundary between the foundational quantum spin Hall result and the much wider subsequent topological-insulator field is not self-evident."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B050",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Michele Parrinello",
        "Roberto Car"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Car–Parrinello method joined electronic-structure theory and molecular dynamics in a way that made realistic atomistic simulation of materials and chemical processes far more practical.",
        "attribution": "The method is specifically associated with Car and Parrinello, whose complementary contributions are closely tied to its conception and formulation.",
        "maturity": "It has had decades of use and influence, with extensive methodological development and applications.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The case should weigh the method's lasting impact against the many later computational advances that broadened its reach."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B008",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Heino Falcke",
        "James M. Moran",
        "Shepperd S. Doeleman"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The first horizon-scale black-hole images gave striking observational access to strong-gravity environments and demonstrated the power of Earth-sized very-long-baseline interferometry.",
        "attribution": "Falcke advanced the black-hole shadow proposal, Moran helped establish the relevant millimeter VLBI foundations, and Doeleman led key experimental efforts. The images depended on a large international collaboration.",
        "maturity": "The M87* and Sgr A* results provide repeated imaging evidence, though interpretation and imaging methods continue to develop.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Individual credit is difficult to separate from the many observatories, technical teams, and analysis contributors.",
          "The lasting scientific reach of the images beyond their landmark demonstration remains under development."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B026",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Alan H. Guth",
        "Andrei Linde",
        "Viatcheslav Mukhanov"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Inflation offers an influential account of the universe's early expansion, while the quantum origin of primordial structure connects fundamental theory to measurable cosmic patterns.",
        "attribution": "Guth and Linde developed influential inflationary scenarios; Mukhanov made foundational contributions to the theory of primordial perturbations. Credit across the many models and contributors needs careful delimitation.",
        "maturity": "The predicted structure of primordial fluctuations is strongly connected to observations, but inflation as a physical process has not been uniquely established.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "No distinctive observation yet selects inflation over all alternatives or identifies a particular inflationary model.",
          "The appropriate boundary between early-universe theory and the experimentally supported origin of observed structure is contested."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B027",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Gerard 't Hooft",
        "Juan Maldacena",
        "Leonard Susskind"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The holographic principle and gauge/gravity duality are profound conceptual advances that have reshaped theoretical work on quantum gravity and strongly coupled systems.",
        "attribution": "The named physicists made distinct foundational contributions to holography and its realization as a duality. The development is broad, and overlaps with other formulations of gauge/gravity duality.",
        "maturity": "The mathematical framework is highly developed and fruitful, but its direct application to our universe and experimental confirmation remain uncertain.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The physical status of the duality as a description of nature, rather than a powerful theoretical correspondence, remains unsettled.",
          "Related candidate formulations overlap substantially, making the discovery boundary and credit allocation difficult."
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
